Sure! Here's an example of how you can use OpenCV.js to perform Canny edge detection on an image uploaded via the file reader API in an HTML page:

First, you'll need to include the OpenCV.js library in your HTML file. You can do this by adding the following script tag to your HTML file:

```html
<script async src="https://docs.opencv.org/4.5.0/opencv.js"></script>
```

Next, you'll need to create an HTML form that allows the user to upload an image file. Here's an example of how you can do this:

```html
<input type="file" id="imageUpload" accept="image/*">
<canvas id="canvas"></canvas>
```

Then, you'll need to write JavaScript code that reads the uploaded image file, converts it to a format that OpenCV.js can work with, and applies Canny edge detection to it. Here's an example of how you can do this:

```javascript
const imageUpload = document.getElementById('imageUpload');
const canvas = document.getElementById('canvas');
const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

imageUpload.addEventListener('change', function(event) {
  const file = event.target.files[0];
  const reader = new FileReader();

  reader.onload = function(e) {
    const img = new Image();
    img.onload = function() {
      canvas.width = img.width;
      canvas.height = img.height;
      ctx.drawImage(img, 0, 0);
      processImage();
    };
    img.src = e.target.result;
  };

  reader.readAsDataURL(file);
});

function processImage() {
  const src = cv.imread(canvas);
  const dst = new cv.Mat();
  const gray = new cv.Mat();
  const edges = new cv.Mat();

  cv.cvtColor(src, gray, cv.COLOR_RGBA2GRAY, 0);
  cv.Canny(gray, edges, 100, 200);

  cv.imshow('canvas', edges);
  src.delete();
  gray.delete();
  edges.delete();
}
```

In this example, when the user selects an image file, it is read using the FileReader API and converted to a data URL. The image is then loaded into an HTML canvas element, and the `processImage()` function is called. This function uses OpenCV.js to convert the image to grayscale, apply Canny edge detection, and display the resulting edges on the canvas.

Note that you'll need to make sure that the canvas element has an ID of "canvas" and that the OpenCV.js library is loaded before the script runs. Also, the `cv.imshow()` function is used to display the processed image on the canvas.

